1
00:00:01,375 --> 00:00:03,002
"Previously on
The Mentalist..."

2
00:00:03,127 --> 00:00:04,170
This is my date outfit.

3
00:00:04,295 --> 00:00:06,756
Is this your first date? I mean
um, since your wife died.

4
00:00:06,881 --> 00:00:08,049
Do I seem outta practice?

5
00:00:08,216 --> 00:00:11,135
Red John
if you're listening to us now

6
00:00:11,260 --> 00:00:13,971
I urge you to reach out
and get help.

7
00:00:14,096 --> 00:00:15,139
You can change.

8
00:00:15,264 --> 00:00:16,307
"What the hell are you thinking?"

9
00:00:16,432 --> 00:00:17,975
"Don't you understand
who you're dealing with?"

10
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:20,353
- Red John is not coming after me.
- Are you blind?

11
00:00:20,478 --> 00:00:23,272
You've done exactly what I did
and he killed my wife and child.

12
00:00:23,397 --> 00:00:24,440
Kristina?

13
00:00:25,107 --> 00:00:27,068
"Red John could've gotten
in and taken her"

14
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:29,403
"or maybe
he lured her out somehow."

15
00:00:31,489 --> 00:00:34,075
"Tiger, tiger burning bright"

16
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:36,410
"in the forests of the night"

17
00:00:36,577 --> 00:00:40,998
"what immortal hand or eye
could frame thy fearful symmetry?"

18
00:00:42,750 --> 00:00:46,379
I almost forgot. Kristina would
want me to send her love.

19
00:01:01,852 --> 00:01:03,854
- You can't park there.
- Sure, I can.

20
00:01:04,313 --> 00:01:05,356
Look.

21
00:01:12,488 --> 00:01:15,116
- There you are.
- Here I am. Good morning.

22
00:01:15,741 --> 00:01:16,909
Why so glum?

23
00:01:17,326 --> 00:01:19,120
This is Harvey Dublin.

24
00:01:20,162 --> 00:01:23,291
That's his chauffeur Peter Russo
with three bullets in him.

25
00:01:23,416 --> 00:01:25,126
That's his car.
Mr. Dublin's gone.

26
00:01:25,251 --> 00:01:26,586
Abducted, it looks like.

27
00:01:30,923 --> 00:01:33,467
- Harvey Dublin?
- Big-time statehouse lobbyist

28
00:01:33,593 --> 00:01:35,094
lawyer, kingmaker.

29
00:01:36,178 --> 00:01:38,639
- Famous and well-connected.
- Ah, the evil twins.

30
00:01:38,764 --> 00:01:42,268
Brass and media are gonna be
all over us like suckerfish.

31
00:01:42,393 --> 00:01:44,562
Oh, why don't we just give it
to the FBI? They love that stuff.

32
00:01:44,687 --> 00:01:46,314
Oh, I wish. Too late.

33
00:01:46,439 --> 00:01:49,150
The new boss is here doing
on-cameras. It's our baby.

34
00:01:49,317 --> 00:01:50,943
New boss?
I thought Hightower was boss.

35
00:01:51,068 --> 00:01:52,778
The boss of Hightower.

36
00:01:52,987 --> 00:01:56,198
The director of the entire division.
I told you about him last week.

37
00:01:56,699 --> 00:01:57,950
Your memory's going.

38
00:01:58,659 --> 00:02:00,786
My memory is a mighty fortress, Lisbon

39
00:02:00,911 --> 00:02:04,582
from which no fact
ever escapes once committed.

40
00:02:04,749 --> 00:02:06,626
Now when you tell me
boring things

41
00:02:06,917 --> 00:02:08,669
I set 'em free immediately.

42
00:02:09,295 --> 00:02:10,755
Saves overcrowding.

43
00:02:12,465 --> 00:02:14,550
Harvey Dublin is missing.

44
00:02:14,675 --> 00:02:16,302
It looks like a kidnapping

45
00:02:16,427 --> 00:02:20,014
presumably for ransom
but we can't be sure at present.

46
00:02:20,139 --> 00:02:22,308
We shall have to await
further developments.

47
00:02:23,476 --> 00:02:25,353
Harvey is a pillar of this city

48
00:02:25,936 --> 00:02:27,313
one of the good guys.

49
00:02:27,438 --> 00:02:30,274
We are all praying
for his safety.

50
00:02:30,608 --> 00:02:31,651
Thank you.

51
00:02:32,693 --> 00:02:33,736
Idiot.

52
00:02:33,861 --> 00:02:36,405
You and your snap judgments.
What is he supposed to say?

53
00:02:36,530 --> 00:02:38,032
Why does he have to say anything?

54
00:02:40,618 --> 00:02:41,911
What's your take on this?

55
00:02:42,953 --> 00:02:44,205
Hmm.

56
00:02:44,580 --> 00:02:45,873
I don't know.

57
00:02:51,128 --> 00:02:52,922
Eh, I think I'll give this one a miss.

58
00:02:53,047 --> 00:02:55,132
A miss?
What do you mean "a miss"?

59
00:02:55,257 --> 00:02:57,760
I just feel this one
is not for me.

60
00:02:58,094 --> 00:03:00,179
I'll catch the next one.
I-I-I promise.

61
00:03:00,304 --> 00:03:01,806
I don't understand.
You're not gonna...

62
00:03:01,931 --> 00:03:05,351
- Agent Lisbon, I believe? And Mr. Jane?
- Yes. Hi.

63
00:03:05,476 --> 00:03:06,977
Director Bertram
will speak to you now.

64
00:03:07,103 --> 00:03:09,605
We're on a super tight schedule.
He appreciates facts and brevity.

65
00:03:09,730 --> 00:03:10,898
Okay?

66
00:03:11,023 --> 00:03:14,068
- Agent Lisbon.
- Yes, sir, Director Bertram.

67
00:03:14,735 --> 00:03:16,862
And you must be our boy wonder
Patrick Jane.

68
00:03:16,987 --> 00:03:18,698
I've heard a lotta good things
about you.

69
00:03:18,823 --> 00:03:20,324
Hi. Nice to meet you, sir.

70
00:03:20,449 --> 00:03:22,952
Now I want this resolved
A.S.A.P.

71
00:03:23,077 --> 00:03:25,705
Whatever it takes. The heck
with overtime restrictions.

72
00:03:25,830 --> 00:03:27,832
You do the hours
I'll find the funding.

73
00:03:27,957 --> 00:03:30,292
We are all in this together
as one.

74
00:03:30,418 --> 00:03:32,795
- That's what I'm all about.
- Oh, we are on it, sir.

75
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:34,714
Uh, yeah, I won't be working
this particular case

76
00:03:34,839 --> 00:03:36,298
but I-I do like what you said.

77
00:03:36,424 --> 00:03:38,926
We're all in this together as one.
Mao Tse Tung, right?

78
00:03:39,051 --> 00:03:40,469
Now hold your horses there, Buster.

79
00:03:40,594 --> 00:03:42,012
What do you mean you won't
be working on this one?

80
00:03:42,555 --> 00:03:45,057
Well, statehouse lobbyist
abductions...

81
00:03:45,182 --> 00:03:47,435
it's, uh, not really
my cup of tea.

82
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:50,563
Well, that's... that's kind of
unprofessional, don't you think?

83
00:03:50,688 --> 00:03:53,065
I guess, but I'm not actually
a professional.

84
00:03:53,858 --> 00:03:55,901
Question for you.
W-why are you here?

85
00:03:56,026 --> 00:03:57,361
Aside from the cameras.

86
00:03:57,945 --> 00:04:00,698
Oh, what do you mean
"aside from the cameras"?

87
00:04:00,823 --> 00:04:02,742
Public perception
is an important tool.

88
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:04,243
Mm.

89
00:04:04,535 --> 00:04:05,578
Tool.

90
00:04:08,122 --> 00:04:10,291
- You just put your hand on me.
- No.

91
00:04:10,583 --> 00:04:12,793
- I mean, yes, but...
- Yes, you did. Don't ever do that again.

92
00:04:13,794 --> 00:04:15,087
Or else what?

93
00:04:18,591 --> 00:04:21,635
You ever smell a dead mouse?
Do you remember that smell?

94
00:04:22,011 --> 00:04:23,137
Yes.

95
00:04:23,262 --> 00:04:26,140
Well, I just now put a dead mouse
in your jacket pocket.

96
00:04:26,515 --> 00:04:29,435
And no matter how many times
you clean that jacket

97
00:04:29,685 --> 00:04:31,604
you'll always smell that dead mouse

98
00:04:31,729 --> 00:04:35,900
and you'll remember that you should
never have put your hand on me.

99
00:04:40,821 --> 00:04:42,031
You son of a bitch.

100
00:04:42,156 --> 00:04:43,574
It's actually just a wadded up napkin

101
00:04:43,699 --> 00:04:45,951
but, uh, has the same
psychological effect.

102
00:04:46,410 --> 00:04:48,287
Human mind is funny that way.

103
00:04:48,954 --> 00:04:51,582
Good to meet you, sir.
Sorry, Lisbon.

104
00:04:55,252 --> 00:04:56,796
He had a rough summer.

105
00:05:10,432 --> 00:05:13,560
"Rigsby and Van Pelt are canvassing
the area around the abduction site."

106
00:05:13,685 --> 00:05:15,979
Cho's with Mrs. Dublin
waiting for a ransom demand

107
00:05:16,104 --> 00:05:17,773
and tech services
are up and running.

108
00:05:18,398 --> 00:05:20,818
Well, this is a Scary one.
Lots of attention.

109
00:05:21,652 --> 00:05:22,903
{\an8}Keep Molly in public affairs

110
00:05:23,028 --> 00:05:25,656
{\an8}apprised of any good stuff
we can feed the media

111
00:05:25,781 --> 00:05:27,157
keep them on our side

112
00:05:27,449 --> 00:05:29,117
{\an8}and don't make any mistakes.

113
00:05:29,493 --> 00:05:30,536
{\an8}No, ma'am.

114
00:05:31,286 --> 00:05:32,538
How's Jane?

115
00:05:34,623 --> 00:05:35,749
{\an8}The same.

116
00:05:35,958 --> 00:05:37,793
You think he's in danger
of cracking up?

117
00:05:38,335 --> 00:05:39,461
No.

118
00:05:41,255 --> 00:05:44,883
{\an8}I mean, no. Well, I don't think so.
I think he'll be fine.

119
00:05:47,052 --> 00:05:48,720
{\an8}That's not very convincing, Lisbon.

120
00:05:50,430 --> 00:05:52,558
What he's been through
would break anyone.

121
00:05:52,975 --> 00:05:54,726
{\an8}He's not gonna crack up.

122
00:05:55,727 --> 00:05:57,896
{\an8}I think some time off
will do him good, though.

123
00:05:58,272 --> 00:06:00,649
{\an8}Yeah, well, no can do.

124
00:06:01,316 --> 00:06:03,235
{\an8}Bertram wants him
on the Dublin case.

125
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:06,196
{\an8}- After Jane was so rude to him?
- Bertram's all bottom line.

126
00:06:06,321 --> 00:06:08,198
{\an8}He won't let
personal issues intrude.

127
00:06:08,574 --> 00:06:11,368
{\an8}Jane's the best asset we have
and Bertram knows that.

128
00:06:11,493 --> 00:06:14,580
- But Jane doesn't wanna be on the case.
- Change his mind.

129
00:06:15,622 --> 00:06:18,250
{\an8}I'm not gonna tell Bertram
we can't control our own people.

130
00:06:24,464 --> 00:06:26,800
{\an8}I have an idea
as to how to persuade him

131
00:06:26,925 --> 00:06:28,385
{\an8}but it's kind of unethical.

132
00:06:29,386 --> 00:06:30,721
{\an8}I don't wanna know.

133
00:06:38,520 --> 00:06:40,355
{\an8}- Where's Jane?
- He's upstairs.

134
00:06:40,564 --> 00:06:41,607
{\an8}Thanks.

135
00:06:53,452 --> 00:06:54,494
Jane?

136
00:06:55,287 --> 00:06:56,371
{\an8}Come in.

137
00:06:57,205 --> 00:06:58,415
{\an8}Hey.

138
00:06:58,790 --> 00:07:00,125
Nice place.

139
00:07:00,250 --> 00:07:04,463
{\an8}Yeah. Yeah, I like
the view and the quiet.

140
00:07:08,926 --> 00:07:10,844
And what are you doing
with all the quiet?

141
00:07:11,637 --> 00:07:13,013
{\an8}Just thinking.

142
00:07:14,890 --> 00:07:17,017
{\an8}Jane, you have to stop
blaming yourself.

143
00:07:17,142 --> 00:07:18,602
{\an8}If Red John took Kristina Frye
it's not your...

144
00:07:18,727 --> 00:07:22,940
{\an8}- There's no "if", he took her.
- If he did, you can't blame yourself.

145
00:07:23,398 --> 00:07:25,067
{\an8}Besides, what good is it
to sit up here

146
00:07:25,192 --> 00:07:28,320
{\an8}scribbling in your book?
You're not gonna find her that way.

147
00:07:32,366 --> 00:07:35,452
I'm not gonna change my mind.
I don't wanna work the Dublin case.

148
00:07:36,119 --> 00:07:38,747
That's fine. It's a done deal.
I understand.

149
00:07:39,164 --> 00:07:40,832
It'd be good for you
to have a break.

150
00:07:43,085 --> 00:07:45,587
Listen, on a completely other subject

151
00:07:45,712 --> 00:07:47,255
I need a personal favor.

152
00:07:48,924 --> 00:07:50,717
I'd like you to come
and meet someone.

153
00:07:50,842 --> 00:07:51,885
Who?

154
00:07:52,052 --> 00:07:53,929
- I can't tell.
- Why not?

155
00:07:54,137 --> 00:07:57,474
I promise it has nothing to do
with Harvey Dublin.

156
00:07:57,933 --> 00:07:59,726
You're trying to play me somehow

157
00:07:59,851 --> 00:08:01,520
convince me
to change my mind.

158
00:08:01,645 --> 00:08:03,605
- No.
- Yes, you are.

159
00:08:05,273 --> 00:08:06,733
And I guess I owe you a shot.

160
00:08:06,858 --> 00:08:08,777
But it's not gonna work
whatever it is.

161
00:08:20,330 --> 00:08:22,791
Hey, Cho, how's it goin'
with Mrs. Dublin?

162
00:08:23,250 --> 00:08:25,836
Nothing so far. When's somebody
coming to relieve me here?

163
00:08:26,003 --> 00:08:27,212
Why? What's the problem?

164
00:08:27,421 --> 00:08:29,381
Nothing. I'll call if we get
any developments.

165
00:08:30,716 --> 00:08:33,468
- What is it? Is it Harvey?
- No, ma'am.

166
00:08:33,844 --> 00:08:35,929
Oh, my God!

167
00:08:36,138 --> 00:08:38,890
I am gonna explode!
I can't stand it!

168
00:08:39,182 --> 00:08:42,060
I can't take it!
I swear to God!

169
00:08:43,729 --> 00:08:45,105
Why, Kimball?

170
00:08:46,231 --> 00:08:49,443
Why am I being punished?
I'm a good person.

171
00:08:50,235 --> 00:08:51,611
Yes, ma'am.

172
00:08:51,945 --> 00:08:55,574
Stop calling me ma'am!
I'm not your grandmother.

173
00:08:56,033 --> 00:08:58,243
How old do you think I am
anyway?

174
00:08:59,411 --> 00:09:01,163
48, 49.

175
00:09:10,047 --> 00:09:11,882
- I get the picture.
- Do you?

176
00:09:12,507 --> 00:09:16,636
Yeah, Dublin's driver...
the dead guy, Pete Russo, right?

177
00:09:16,887 --> 00:09:18,221
He has family.

178
00:09:18,430 --> 00:09:21,183
He wasn't wearing
a wedding ring, so, uh...

179
00:09:21,308 --> 00:09:22,934
We're about to meet a, uh...

180
00:09:25,520 --> 00:09:27,439
Yes. Exactly.

181
00:09:28,690 --> 00:09:30,025
Yes, who are you?

182
00:09:30,776 --> 00:09:32,861
Nadine, I'm Agent Lisbon
with the CBI.

183
00:09:32,986 --> 00:09:34,321
We spoke on the phone.

184
00:09:34,529 --> 00:09:35,822
This is Patrick Jane.

185
00:09:35,947 --> 00:09:38,408
Hi. You're, uh,
Pete Russo's daughter?

186
00:09:38,867 --> 00:09:40,077
Uh, yeah.

187
00:09:41,745 --> 00:09:43,288
- Hello?
- They're police.

188
00:09:43,872 --> 00:09:45,874
- Oh, okay.
- This is my mom.

189
00:09:46,166 --> 00:09:47,459
Patricia.

190
00:09:48,627 --> 00:09:50,295
And this is Keith, my stepdad.

191
00:09:50,712 --> 00:09:52,130
Hi. Um...

192
00:09:52,923 --> 00:09:54,174
Please come in.

193
00:10:03,975 --> 00:10:06,436
Miss Lisbon says you're the best
detective in California.

194
00:10:10,398 --> 00:10:13,026
She says if anybody can find
who killed my father, you can.

195
00:10:15,445 --> 00:10:16,488
I just have to say

196
00:10:16,613 --> 00:10:18,990
I didn't think you would stoop
to such crude manipulation.

197
00:10:20,408 --> 00:10:21,743
I don't understand.

198
00:10:22,410 --> 00:10:24,079
Inside joke. I'm sorry.

199
00:10:24,204 --> 00:10:27,582
Uh, I will find who killed your father
and abducted Mr. Dublin.

200
00:10:27,749 --> 00:10:29,042
An inside joke?

201
00:10:29,918 --> 00:10:31,253
Her father is dead.

202
00:10:31,920 --> 00:10:32,963
Murdered.

203
00:10:33,755 --> 00:10:35,257
You're making jokes?

204
00:10:36,925 --> 00:10:40,720
Yes, uh, it's a fault of mine.
Uh, what it is, the joke?

205
00:10:40,846 --> 00:10:44,182
Uh, I have a... I have a lot
on my mind right now.

206
00:10:44,307 --> 00:10:46,268
Uh, I didn't really wanna come
to work today

207
00:10:46,393 --> 00:10:48,854
but my good friend and colleague
Agent Lisbon

208
00:10:49,020 --> 00:10:51,398
tricked me into meeting you

209
00:10:51,857 --> 00:10:53,275
knowing that I couldn't refuse you

210
00:10:53,400 --> 00:10:54,943
because you're the age
my daughter would be

211
00:10:55,068 --> 00:10:57,320
if she were still alive
if she hadn't been murdered.

212
00:10:58,029 --> 00:11:00,782
- Oh, goodness.
- Murdered by a man named Red John

213
00:11:00,907 --> 00:11:02,200
whom I met with recently.

214
00:11:02,325 --> 00:11:04,286
He saved my life
and abducted a friend of mine.

215
00:11:04,619 --> 00:11:08,081
Long story.
Hence, many things on my mind.

216
00:11:10,625 --> 00:11:12,419
That's not a very funny joke.

217
00:11:13,128 --> 00:11:14,337
Hmm.

218
00:11:15,172 --> 00:11:16,673
What happened to your friend?

219
00:11:16,798 --> 00:11:19,676
Still missing.
Uh, please, tell us about your father.

220
00:11:22,762 --> 00:11:24,347
He was a good person.

221
00:11:25,807 --> 00:11:28,768
He was... funny and...

222
00:11:29,686 --> 00:11:31,104
And generous.

223
00:11:31,229 --> 00:11:33,857
And I could talk to him about
anything, and he would listen.

224
00:11:34,274 --> 00:11:36,484
Yes, he could be charming.

225
00:11:38,987 --> 00:11:40,447
He had his ways.

226
00:11:40,947 --> 00:11:44,159
- What kinda ways?
- He had a temper.

227
00:11:44,868 --> 00:11:46,203
That he did.

228
00:11:48,079 --> 00:11:50,498
I mean, his heart was
in the right place, you know?

229
00:11:53,001 --> 00:11:54,586
Whatever his faults

230
00:11:54,920 --> 00:11:56,838
he didn't deserve to die this way.

231
00:11:58,840 --> 00:12:00,008
Really, I'm impressed.

232
00:12:00,133 --> 00:12:02,802
You... you played me like a fish
and... and you lied to me.

233
00:12:02,928 --> 00:12:04,679
I kinda did, didn't I? Sorry.

234
00:12:04,804 --> 00:12:06,848
No, I'm glad to see you're finally
learning a few things.

235
00:12:06,973 --> 00:12:09,059
Of course, uh, now I shall have
my revenge.

236
00:12:09,184 --> 00:12:10,268
What do you mean?

237
00:12:14,898 --> 00:12:16,942
Don't worry.
We're not here about drugs.

238
00:12:17,067 --> 00:12:18,526
We're here about the killing
the other night.

239
00:12:18,652 --> 00:12:20,654
Ah, yeah, yeah.
I heard about that.

240
00:12:20,987 --> 00:12:23,073
Kidnapped a man, huh?
That's too bad.

241
00:12:23,198 --> 00:12:25,450
Local beat cops say you guys
do business under that bridge.

242
00:12:25,575 --> 00:12:28,662
Yeah, look, the local beat cops
are nice guys, but they're outta touch.

243
00:12:29,037 --> 00:12:32,165
We don't do business under that bridge.
The hos... they got dibs.

244
00:12:32,415 --> 00:12:34,876
You're gonna wanna talk
to the ladies over on Fremont.

245
00:12:35,543 --> 00:12:37,504
- All right. Thanks.
- Yeah, no worries.

246
00:12:38,880 --> 00:12:40,048
Hey, hey, Red.

247
00:12:40,173 --> 00:12:41,925
You... you ever date
a hustler before?

248
00:12:42,550 --> 00:12:43,760
Not so far!

249
00:12:44,844 --> 00:12:46,137
What? I'm kidding.

250
00:12:51,226 --> 00:12:52,936
- Thank you.
- Yeah. Okay.

251
00:12:53,061 --> 00:12:54,104
Good to know.

252
00:12:55,272 --> 00:12:58,483
Good afternoon. Reid Colman.
I'm Mr. Dublin's personal lawyer.

253
00:12:58,608 --> 00:13:01,069
Glad to see you people.
You made any progress?

254
00:13:01,403 --> 00:13:03,321
Early days, Mr. Colman.

255
00:13:03,738 --> 00:13:06,032
You'll, uh, need to talk to me, yes?

256
00:13:06,574 --> 00:13:08,201
Now's a good time.
I have ten minutes or so.

257
00:13:08,326 --> 00:13:10,412
If you'll come this way
we can use the conference room.

258
00:13:11,162 --> 00:13:12,455
Uh, just a moment.

259
00:13:13,790 --> 00:13:15,041
What's your name?

260
00:13:15,542 --> 00:13:17,460
- Marjorie.
- Marjorie.

261
00:13:17,585 --> 00:13:20,380
That's a very nice name. I'd like
to speak to Marjorie first, thanks.

262
00:13:20,505 --> 00:13:22,716
Uh, we will need to speak
to you in due course, sir.

263
00:13:22,841 --> 00:13:24,676
I'll, uh, try and fit you in later.

264
00:13:25,135 --> 00:13:27,929
Prostitutes? No. No.

265
00:13:28,388 --> 00:13:30,557
Mr. Dublin was not
that sort of man at all.

266
00:13:30,682 --> 00:13:32,392
The alleyway
from which he was abducted

267
00:13:32,517 --> 00:13:34,185
was used by prostitutes

268
00:13:34,311 --> 00:13:36,646
and it was nowhere near
Mr. Dublin's regular route.

269
00:13:36,771 --> 00:13:39,983
I have been Mr. Dublin's personal
secretary for ten years

270
00:13:40,191 --> 00:13:42,068
and I can tell you
without a shadow of a doubt

271
00:13:42,193 --> 00:13:44,362
that he is the most decent

272
00:13:45,405 --> 00:13:48,199
caring, clean, generous human being

273
00:13:48,325 --> 00:13:49,909
that anyone could hope
to meet.

274
00:13:50,076 --> 00:13:52,495
We wanna bring him back
safely, Marjorie.

275
00:13:52,620 --> 00:13:53,830
We need to know everything.

276
00:14:01,671 --> 00:14:04,466
Usually I can get ahold
of him at any time.

277
00:14:04,758 --> 00:14:07,093
His work demands it
but once in a while

278
00:14:07,427 --> 00:14:09,346
he'd disappear for an hour or so

279
00:14:09,971 --> 00:14:12,432
no... no answer from him
or his driver.

280
00:14:12,557 --> 00:14:14,768
- How often was that?
- Every couple of weeks.

281
00:14:15,101 --> 00:14:18,146
A regular date right around
the time he was abducted, yes?

282
00:14:21,399 --> 00:14:23,318
And what did you think
he might be doing?

283
00:14:23,693 --> 00:14:25,236
Mistress, I thought

284
00:14:26,196 --> 00:14:27,614
but not this.

285
00:14:28,281 --> 00:14:30,367
Men have needs.
I understand that.

286
00:14:32,077 --> 00:14:34,079
Please, you'll find him, won't you?

287
00:14:34,537 --> 00:14:35,789
You'll find him?

288
00:14:35,914 --> 00:14:37,540
Marjorie, uh, I have to tell you

289
00:14:37,665 --> 00:14:39,918
it's very likely
that he's already dead.

290
00:14:40,085 --> 00:14:41,795
Whoa. Wait a minute.

291
00:14:42,587 --> 00:14:44,089
- We don't know that.
- It's a hunch that I have

292
00:14:44,214 --> 00:14:45,548
and if I'm right

293
00:14:45,673 --> 00:14:47,050
it's a blessing for you.

294
00:14:47,967 --> 00:14:49,594
Unrequited love
is a terrible thing.

295
00:14:49,886 --> 00:14:52,555
You have to find yourself a man
that will love you back.

296
00:14:53,765 --> 00:14:57,018
You're a good woman.
You deserve it. Think about that.

297
00:15:07,237 --> 00:15:09,989
So this was done by someone
who knew about Dublin's routine...

298
00:15:10,115 --> 00:15:11,658
someone very close.

299
00:15:12,409 --> 00:15:13,952
Wh-why did you say
he was dead?

300
00:15:14,077 --> 00:15:16,413
Because he is. He's dead
and it's not a kidnapping.

301
00:15:16,538 --> 00:15:17,580
Give me a reason.

302
00:15:17,705 --> 00:15:18,957
I'll bet you all the tea in China.

303
00:15:19,332 --> 00:15:21,292
- That is not a reason.
- I'll give you good odds.

304
00:15:21,501 --> 00:15:22,544
Hey.

305
00:15:22,669 --> 00:15:24,295
Hey. Ransom demand
came through.

306
00:15:24,504 --> 00:15:25,547
How much?

307
00:15:26,297 --> 00:15:27,715
All right.
We're on our way.

308
00:15:29,342 --> 00:15:32,178
A million dollars
for the safe return of Dublin...

309
00:15:32,303 --> 00:15:34,472
that kinda sounds like
a kidnapping, doesn't it?

310
00:15:34,639 --> 00:15:37,183
Doesn't mean it's a kidnapping.
Doesn't mean he's not dead.

311
00:15:37,600 --> 00:15:39,811
Is this a serious theory
or is this your revenge?

312
00:15:55,007 --> 00:15:56,509
{\an8}"This was sent
from a throwaway phone."

313
00:15:56,634 --> 00:15:58,844
"Do exactly as instructed
or H.D. gets his throat cut"

314
00:15:58,970 --> 00:16:00,388
"and we send you his tongue."

315
00:16:00,513 --> 00:16:02,348
"We want a million dollars
in used 50s and 100s"

316
00:16:02,473 --> 00:16:03,683
"at 0600 tomorrow."

317
00:16:03,808 --> 00:16:06,561
"Location will follow.
No police, or H.D. is dead."

318
00:16:07,186 --> 00:16:08,688
- Any proof of life?
- No.

319
00:16:09,772 --> 00:16:10,815
Huh.

320
00:16:11,107 --> 00:16:13,651
Do not say anything bad
to Mrs. Dublin.

321
00:16:13,776 --> 00:16:14,944
That is an order.

322
00:16:15,069 --> 00:16:16,487
"Jawohl, mein Kommandant."

323
00:16:17,613 --> 00:16:19,031
- Can I see?
- Yeah.

324
00:16:19,365 --> 00:16:20,658
Where is Mrs. Dublin?

325
00:16:21,284 --> 00:16:23,536
Yeah, she cries a lot.
Then she has to freshen her makeup

326
00:16:23,661 --> 00:16:25,538
and then she cries again.
Sort of a cycle.

327
00:16:25,871 --> 00:16:29,667
Three deliberate misspellings
and an obvious military lilt

328
00:16:29,792 --> 00:16:32,461
which suggests
that the writer is not a veteran

329
00:16:32,795 --> 00:16:35,006
but wants us to believe
that he or she is...

330
00:16:35,131 --> 00:16:38,884
so a notch or two less clever
than he or she thinks that they are

331
00:16:39,010 --> 00:16:43,347
using this crime to express some
kind of deep, personal grudge.

332
00:16:44,098 --> 00:16:46,267
The violence
is authentic enough.

333
00:16:46,392 --> 00:16:48,603
Oh, my lord. What now?
What's happened?

334
00:16:48,728 --> 00:16:50,104
It's nothing, Giselle.
These are my colleagues.

335
00:16:50,229 --> 00:16:51,480
Please remain calm.

336
00:16:52,106 --> 00:16:53,858
How can I remain calm?

337
00:16:53,983 --> 00:16:57,278
I can't raise
a million dollars by tomorrow!

338
00:16:57,570 --> 00:16:59,488
We aren't nearly as rich
as people think.

339
00:17:00,948 --> 00:17:02,491
Wh-what do I do?

340
00:17:02,617 --> 00:17:05,119
I can't speak
to the financial aspects, ma'am.

341
00:17:05,244 --> 00:17:06,370
That's your call.

342
00:17:06,495 --> 00:17:08,789
Now can you think of anybody
who may have had a personal grudge

343
00:17:08,914 --> 00:17:10,082
against your husband?

344
00:17:10,666 --> 00:17:12,877
No. Nobody.

345
00:17:13,210 --> 00:17:18,174
My husband is a respected and beloved
member of Sacramento society.

346
00:17:18,299 --> 00:17:19,342
And you?

347
00:17:20,509 --> 00:17:23,262
Do you respect and belove him?

348
00:17:23,888 --> 00:17:25,431
Of course I do.

349
00:17:25,931 --> 00:17:28,768
Deeply. What a question.

350
00:17:29,393 --> 00:17:32,229
Well, wh-why don't you wanna raise
a million dollars to save his life?

351
00:17:32,647 --> 00:17:35,024
Why do you ask that? I do!

352
00:17:35,149 --> 00:17:38,110
Well, the watch and the earrings...
that's, like, a quarter mill right there.

353
00:17:38,653 --> 00:17:40,363
You can raise the money.

354
00:17:41,447 --> 00:17:44,575
You're conflicted because you don't
know if you want your husband back.

355
00:17:44,742 --> 00:17:46,494
You don't like your husband
very much at all.

356
00:17:47,244 --> 00:17:48,663
You're actually a very good actress

357
00:17:49,789 --> 00:17:51,749
playing the part of the loving wife.

358
00:17:51,874 --> 00:17:53,376
That's what I think you are.

359
00:17:53,542 --> 00:17:56,212
Can you look at me
and tell me that I'm wrong?

360
00:18:01,884 --> 00:18:03,135
Duh.

361
00:18:05,721 --> 00:18:08,307
Of course I don't like him
very much.

362
00:18:09,183 --> 00:18:11,102
Everybody in the know
knows that Harvey Dublin

363
00:18:11,227 --> 00:18:15,815
is a mean, lying, backstabbing
crooked son of a bitch.

364
00:18:16,023 --> 00:18:19,318
But I'm his wife and what am I supposed
to do but act like I love him?

365
00:18:19,652 --> 00:18:21,112
Why didn't you leave him?

366
00:18:21,737 --> 00:18:23,864
Have you ever divorced
a mean lawyer?

367
00:18:24,699 --> 00:18:27,076
He has no record of any
personal wrongdoing.

368
00:18:27,201 --> 00:18:28,953
He's a respected lobbyist.

369
00:18:29,078 --> 00:18:31,539
An extortionist and con man
more like it.

370
00:18:31,664 --> 00:18:34,375
So there are a lotta people who may
have wanted to harm your husband.

371
00:18:34,583 --> 00:18:35,876
Are you kidding?

372
00:18:36,335 --> 00:18:39,004
There's people all over the city
praying that he stays gone.

373
00:18:39,130 --> 00:18:40,297
How about you?

374
00:18:40,506 --> 00:18:42,466
- Me?
- Yeah, you.

375
00:18:42,675 --> 00:18:44,677
Oh, I'll raise the money.

376
00:18:45,261 --> 00:18:46,762
I owe him that much.

377
00:18:47,430 --> 00:18:49,056
I knew what I was marrying.

378
00:18:49,223 --> 00:18:52,017
Yes, Mr. Dublin is the target
of some lawsuits.

379
00:18:52,143 --> 00:18:55,187
Most prominent men are at one point
or another in their careers.

380
00:18:55,479 --> 00:18:57,189
It's a hazard of modern life.

381
00:18:57,565 --> 00:18:59,608
So Harvey Dublin's a saint.
That's your position?

382
00:18:59,734 --> 00:19:01,736
No, Harvey Dublin
is not a saint

383
00:19:01,861 --> 00:19:03,863
but I'm his lawyer
and I speak for him.

384
00:19:04,280 --> 00:19:06,282
Look, Harvey started
with less than nothing.

385
00:19:06,407 --> 00:19:09,076
He was a street kid.
His parents were drug addicts.

386
00:19:09,243 --> 00:19:11,495
He lives in fear of falling back
into that world

387
00:19:11,620 --> 00:19:14,165
so maybe he pushes
a little bit too hard.

388
00:19:14,290 --> 00:19:17,585
Maybe he's just a little bit
too intense in his desire to win.

389
00:19:17,752 --> 00:19:19,336
This is America.
That's no crime.

390
00:19:19,545 --> 00:19:21,046
Seven lawsuits pending.

391
00:19:21,172 --> 00:19:23,591
Look, anyone can get sued.
It doesn't mean a thing.

392
00:19:23,716 --> 00:19:26,135
Half the players in this town
are in bed with Dublin

393
00:19:26,260 --> 00:19:27,303
one way or another.

394
00:19:27,428 --> 00:19:29,764
In fact, your, uh, your new director
Gale Bertram...

395
00:19:29,889 --> 00:19:31,098
he's one of his codefendants.

396
00:19:31,807 --> 00:19:33,142
- Really?
- Yeah.

397
00:19:33,267 --> 00:19:36,979
Bank of Claremont vs. Brixham Lake
Development Corporation and others.

398
00:19:37,104 --> 00:19:39,398
- It's gonna be a blockbuster.
- Do go on.

399
00:19:40,232 --> 00:19:43,778
It's a complex land-water
rights thing. It'll run for years.

400
00:19:43,903 --> 00:19:46,530
The point is, Bertram
was named codefendant

401
00:19:46,655 --> 00:19:48,783
to put pressure on the defense
to settle early.

402
00:19:49,116 --> 00:19:50,451
Happens all the time.

403
00:19:50,951 --> 00:19:53,287
Hmm. That's interesting about Bertram.

404
00:19:53,454 --> 00:19:55,164
- No, it's not.
- Yes, it is.

405
00:19:56,290 --> 00:19:59,919
Who's to gain the most legally speaking
if Mr. Dublin doesn't return?

406
00:20:00,044 --> 00:20:01,796
Well, that's, uh,
that's hard to say.

407
00:20:02,171 --> 00:20:03,839
Too many variables
in the equation.

408
00:20:04,173 --> 00:20:05,299
But he is coming back, right?

409
00:20:05,424 --> 00:20:08,219
I mean, if Giselle pays the kidnappers
why shouldn't he?

410
00:20:08,344 --> 00:20:09,470
Well, stuff happens.

411
00:20:09,637 --> 00:20:11,722
Uh, did you ever serve
in the military, Mr. Colman?

412
00:20:12,181 --> 00:20:14,767
No, I didn't have that honor.
Why do you ask?

413
00:20:15,434 --> 00:20:16,644
Standard question.

414
00:20:17,978 --> 00:20:20,439
Don't just...
Oh, shoot.

415
00:20:21,732 --> 00:20:22,942
Hey, lady, wait up.

416
00:20:23,442 --> 00:20:24,819
Ma'am, we just wanna talk.

417
00:20:25,402 --> 00:20:27,571
- Blah, blah, blah, blah.
- Yeah, blah, blah, blah.

418
00:20:27,696 --> 00:20:29,448
Listen, did you hear about that guy
that was kidnapped

419
00:20:29,573 --> 00:20:30,908
up the street a couple blocks over?

420
00:20:31,075 --> 00:20:34,537
- His driver was shot dead.
- Oh, that. Yeah. Isn't that wild?

421
00:20:34,787 --> 00:20:37,373
- Everybody's talking about it.
- What exactly are people saying?

422
00:20:37,498 --> 00:20:40,000
All kinds of stuff.
Friend of mine said she was there.

423
00:20:40,125 --> 00:20:42,628
- Oh, yeah?
- Well, she's acting all mysterious

424
00:20:42,753 --> 00:20:44,964
like, "I can't tell you
what happened"

425
00:20:45,089 --> 00:20:48,050
- like she's a secret agent or whatnot.
- What's your friend's name?

426
00:20:48,551 --> 00:20:50,052
Yours for 50.

427
00:20:52,429 --> 00:20:53,639
This is a murder investigation.

428
00:20:53,764 --> 00:20:55,266
We could take you in
right now for obstruction.

429
00:20:55,391 --> 00:20:57,726
Oh, baby, save yourself time
and paperwork.

430
00:20:57,935 --> 00:21:01,105
I got 40... 43 bucks.

431
00:21:06,026 --> 00:21:08,737
- Twelve cents.
- Money's tight all over, huh?

432
00:21:09,655 --> 00:21:11,323
Come on.
Cut us a break here.

433
00:21:17,371 --> 00:21:20,499
Sugar... big old tall girl, pink hair.

434
00:21:20,624 --> 00:21:22,042
She'll be around tomorrow night.

435
00:21:22,334 --> 00:21:23,836
Nice doin' business with ya.

436
00:21:25,087 --> 00:21:28,465
- Twelve cents? Seriously?
- What? She cleaned me out.

437
00:21:28,716 --> 00:21:29,925
Twelve cents.

438
00:21:35,222 --> 00:21:36,599
Ah.

439
00:21:37,349 --> 00:21:39,059
Interesting about Bertram, huh?

440
00:21:39,184 --> 00:21:41,645
You said that before.
No, it's not.

441
00:21:43,063 --> 00:21:45,399
I got a weird vibe
the first moment I met him.

442
00:21:45,524 --> 00:21:47,943
Uh, the director
of the division of law enforcement

443
00:21:48,068 --> 00:21:50,029
did not kidnap Harvey Dublin.

444
00:21:50,487 --> 00:21:52,615
So you think.
How well do you know him?

445
00:21:52,990 --> 00:21:54,450
This is your revenge, isn't it?

446
00:21:54,575 --> 00:21:56,285
Well, I-I am simply
gonna ignore you.

447
00:21:56,410 --> 00:21:58,787
I'm not punishing you
though you do deserve it.

448
00:22:00,372 --> 00:22:03,208
Dublin turned out to be
the sleazebag I guessed he was.

449
00:22:03,334 --> 00:22:05,461
He deserves justice
like everybody else.

450
00:22:05,586 --> 00:22:07,630
Oh, you're a cop.
You have to say that.

451
00:22:08,005 --> 00:22:10,716
- You don't really believe it.
- My fingers are in my ears.

452
00:22:39,078 --> 00:22:40,120
Morning!

453
00:22:41,914 --> 00:22:42,957
Hey!

454
00:22:43,123 --> 00:22:44,166
What's happening?

455
00:22:44,667 --> 00:22:47,086
- Nothing yet.
- No kidding. Where's the money?

456
00:22:47,836 --> 00:22:49,088
Trash can down there.

457
00:22:49,463 --> 00:22:50,714
- There?
- Yeah.

458
00:22:50,923 --> 00:22:54,051
Oh. Just as I thought.
No one's gonna show up.

459
00:22:54,176 --> 00:22:56,720
Look at this place. It's far too open
for a ransom drop.

460
00:22:57,638 --> 00:22:58,681
Well, they said no police

461
00:22:58,806 --> 00:23:00,849
and maybe they're stupid enough
to think that there's no police.

462
00:23:00,975 --> 00:23:02,059
They killed a guy
in the street.

463
00:23:02,184 --> 00:23:03,769
They have to know
the police are involved.

464
00:23:04,603 --> 00:23:06,563
They never intended
to pick up the ransom.

465
00:23:07,648 --> 00:23:09,358
Well, my hunch is correct.

466
00:23:09,942 --> 00:23:11,318
The kidnapping's a sham.

467
00:23:11,443 --> 00:23:12,486
Dublin's already dead.

468
00:23:12,611 --> 00:23:14,196
What would be the reason
for a pretend kidnapping?

469
00:23:14,321 --> 00:23:16,281
Well, that is the salient question, yes.

470
00:23:16,407 --> 00:23:18,200
But you can stop wasting
your time here, certainly.

471
00:23:18,325 --> 00:23:20,953
Hey, did Mrs. Dublin use
real money or fake money?

472
00:23:21,203 --> 00:23:23,080
- Real.
- Did you look at it?

473
00:23:24,331 --> 00:23:26,834
No. What would that matter now?

474
00:23:26,959 --> 00:23:29,586
Well, it doesn't matter.
I'm... you know, I'm just a little curious.

475
00:23:29,753 --> 00:23:32,881
What you're saying might be true
and it might not be.

476
00:23:33,048 --> 00:23:34,633
We still have to sit
on this thing for a little while

477
00:23:34,758 --> 00:23:37,136
to make sure nobody
shows up... couple of hours.

478
00:23:37,302 --> 00:23:40,264
- Well, that's just silly.
- It's not silly. It's professional.

479
00:23:40,889 --> 00:23:41,932
Professional shmuh-essional.

480
00:23:42,057 --> 00:23:43,308
- Hey!
- Jane!

481
00:24:00,367 --> 00:24:01,452
Hey, it's real!

482
00:24:01,577 --> 00:24:03,245
Police! Freeze!
Hands in the air!

483
00:24:03,370 --> 00:24:05,706
Get on your knees!
Do it now! Do it now!

484
00:24:05,956 --> 00:24:06,999
It's okay.

485
00:24:07,791 --> 00:24:08,834
Don't move.

486
00:24:18,659 --> 00:24:21,578
"The director is expecting a full
but concise progress report."

487
00:24:21,787 --> 00:24:23,747
- How's that jacket smell?
- This is a different suit.

488
00:24:23,872 --> 00:24:25,666
Yeah, but the other one's ruined,
isn't it?

489
00:24:27,084 --> 00:24:28,126
Power of suggestion.

490
00:24:28,251 --> 00:24:30,504
Okay, you know what?
Bite me.

491
00:24:36,134 --> 00:24:37,177
So...

492
00:24:37,594 --> 00:24:41,139
- Poor Harvey Dublin, huh?
- Sir, no one picked up the ransom drop

493
00:24:41,848 --> 00:24:44,351
so for that and other reasons

494
00:24:44,476 --> 00:24:47,604
we believe that the kidnapping aspect
of this case is a pretense.

495
00:24:47,729 --> 00:24:50,357
- Masking what?
- That we can't say for sure.

496
00:24:50,482 --> 00:24:51,775
We have promising leads.

497
00:24:51,900 --> 00:24:53,485
Well, I need something to tell
the media.

498
00:24:53,652 --> 00:24:55,153
Tell 'em Harvey Dublin's dead.

499
00:24:55,988 --> 00:24:58,573
Well, I'm so glad you decided
to work on this case, Mr. Jane.

500
00:24:58,699 --> 00:25:00,409
Your directness is bracing.

501
00:25:00,534 --> 00:25:03,829
Of course, procedurally
we're still assuming he's alive

502
00:25:03,954 --> 00:25:06,248
until we have proof
positive otherwise.

503
00:25:06,581 --> 00:25:09,001
Why kill Dublin?
Wh-what's the motive?

504
00:25:09,126 --> 00:25:10,419
Well, two possibilities.

505
00:25:10,544 --> 00:25:13,255
Uh, one... simple robbery.

506
00:25:13,463 --> 00:25:15,007
The culprit was going
after the diamond

507
00:25:15,132 --> 00:25:17,134
that, uh,
Dublin always carries on him.

508
00:25:18,051 --> 00:25:20,053
Diamond? What diamond?

509
00:25:20,178 --> 00:25:23,223
Well, apparently Dublin had a terror
of falling back into poverty

510
00:25:23,348 --> 00:25:26,101
of, uh, dying in a pauper's grave
like his father did

511
00:25:26,226 --> 00:25:29,521
so he always carried
a large diamond with him

512
00:25:29,813 --> 00:25:31,148
just in case.

513
00:25:31,481 --> 00:25:33,316
It's kind of a touching story, isn't it?

514
00:25:33,442 --> 00:25:35,777
So someone staged
this abduction

515
00:25:35,902 --> 00:25:37,779
to steal the diamond.

516
00:25:38,113 --> 00:25:40,949
- Okay. Um, where's your evidence for this?
- Well, no actual evidence.

517
00:25:41,074 --> 00:25:44,119
- That's... that's word on the street.
- It's more like a theory, really.

518
00:25:44,244 --> 00:25:46,079
- Word on the street.
- Jane.

519
00:25:47,039 --> 00:25:48,832
So you have nothing?

520
00:25:51,835 --> 00:25:53,879
You... you said
there were two possibilities?

521
00:25:54,004 --> 00:25:55,464
Oh, the other reason
someone would kill him

522
00:25:55,589 --> 00:25:59,009
is uh... b-b-b-Brixham Lake.

523
00:25:59,384 --> 00:26:01,887
- Uh, wait a second.
- What about Brixham Lake?

524
00:26:02,095 --> 00:26:05,932
Well, it's such a complex little scandal
isn't it?

525
00:26:06,099 --> 00:26:08,518
And it all goes away
if Harvey Dublin is dead.

526
00:26:09,352 --> 00:26:12,814
I am a named codefendant
in one of the Brixham Lake lawsuits.

527
00:26:12,939 --> 00:26:14,816
Yes, I saw that.
Very interesting.

528
00:26:14,941 --> 00:26:16,818
Uh, obviously you're innocent

529
00:26:16,985 --> 00:26:19,571
but, uh, I imagine
that there's a lot of stuff

530
00:26:19,738 --> 00:26:21,114
that you would rather
remain secret

531
00:26:21,239 --> 00:26:23,950
that will come out
if the suit goes to trial.

532
00:26:24,785 --> 00:26:26,995
You had ample motive.

533
00:26:27,287 --> 00:26:29,831
So that's option two?

534
00:26:29,956 --> 00:26:32,000
Me? I'm your suspect?

535
00:26:32,125 --> 00:26:34,461
Wha... What are you playing at?

536
00:26:34,586 --> 00:26:36,588
Well, I w... I was just wondering
why you didn't tell us

537
00:26:37,005 --> 00:26:38,673
this guy was such a sleazebag

538
00:26:38,799 --> 00:26:41,510
and that you are up to your neck
in his grubby business.

539
00:26:41,635 --> 00:26:42,677
That's enough.

540
00:26:42,803 --> 00:26:45,847
Yes, it is.
I don't have time for this.

541
00:26:45,972 --> 00:26:48,767
You shall just have to trust
in my innocence, Mr. Jane.

542
00:26:48,892 --> 00:26:50,143
I'm not good at trust.

543
00:26:51,645 --> 00:26:54,356
I'm really trying
to allow you the latitude

544
00:26:54,481 --> 00:26:57,400
that your rare talents deserve
but you make it very difficult.

545
00:26:57,526 --> 00:26:59,111
You two may go now.

546
00:27:00,028 --> 00:27:01,780
- Hightower, stay.
- Yes, sir.

547
00:27:09,996 --> 00:27:12,374
What the hell was that?
Are you insane?

548
00:27:12,499 --> 00:27:15,127
And where did you come up
with that stupid-ass diamond story?

549
00:27:15,252 --> 00:27:16,419
I don't know what came over me.

550
00:27:16,545 --> 00:27:19,089
I see that man, I get
an immediate urge to wind him up.

551
00:27:19,214 --> 00:27:21,049
- He's the director.
- I'm sorry.

552
00:27:21,925 --> 00:27:23,135
He amuses me.

553
00:27:23,301 --> 00:27:26,680
I'm glad he entertains you because
he's gonna fire you now probably.

554
00:27:26,805 --> 00:27:29,349
No. You guys need me
too much.

555
00:27:32,269 --> 00:27:33,603
I'll catch up.

556
00:27:39,734 --> 00:27:40,819
Excuse me?

557
00:27:41,736 --> 00:27:42,946
What did you tell her?

558
00:27:43,155 --> 00:27:44,447
I was complimenting her
on her jacket.

559
00:27:44,573 --> 00:27:46,741
- It's such a vivid color.
- Liar.

560
00:27:48,410 --> 00:27:50,120
You're gonna call me a liar?

561
00:27:50,370 --> 00:27:52,956
- Oh, I hate you. I do.
- Oh.

562
00:27:53,623 --> 00:27:56,960
We have our best people
on this thing 24/7.

563
00:27:57,419 --> 00:28:00,922
I want to extend my sympathies
to the family of Peter Russo

564
00:28:01,047 --> 00:28:03,425
and... and please join us
in our prayers

565
00:28:03,550 --> 00:28:05,260
for Mr. Dublin's safety.

566
00:28:05,594 --> 00:28:07,220
- Are there questions... briefly, please?
- Yeah.

567
00:28:07,345 --> 00:28:09,890
Are you looking into this being
a sex-related crime maybe?

568
00:28:10,015 --> 00:28:12,350
- I mean, that alleyway is...
- No, there's no sex angle, Jeff...

569
00:28:12,475 --> 00:28:13,602
none that we're aware of.

570
00:28:13,727 --> 00:28:16,062
Sir, do you think there's
a Brixham Lake connection here?

571
00:28:17,898 --> 00:28:19,524
No. I don't think so.

572
00:28:19,649 --> 00:28:22,027
But both you and Dublin are involved
in that case, aren't you?

573
00:28:22,152 --> 00:28:23,278
No, no, no.
Wh-what we're thinking

574
00:28:23,403 --> 00:28:26,031
is it seems
that Mr. Dublin was rumored

575
00:28:26,156 --> 00:28:30,660
to keep a large diamond
with him, on... on his person

576
00:28:30,785 --> 00:28:32,954
and the theft of that diamond
is our current line of thinking

577
00:28:33,079 --> 00:28:34,122
as to motive.

578
00:28:34,247 --> 00:28:36,833
- "What kind of diamond is it?"
- "Uh, big."

579
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:39,085
"It's 2 or 3 carats, I think."

580
00:28:39,753 --> 00:28:41,630
"Why would he carry a diamond
like that around with him?"

581
00:28:41,755 --> 00:28:43,381
"Well, that's a good question, Jeff."

582
00:28:43,506 --> 00:28:46,927
"Uh, perhaps Mr. Dublin
was trying to be prepared"

583
00:28:47,052 --> 00:28:48,595
"for any circumstance.
You know..."

584
00:28:49,763 --> 00:28:52,474
"Understandable, isn't it?
I mean he's, uh, had a tough..."

585
00:28:54,100 --> 00:28:55,268
"Ask just one more question..."

586
00:28:59,981 --> 00:29:02,400
How do you think
the director is going to feel

587
00:29:02,525 --> 00:29:03,860
when he finds out
you fooled him

588
00:29:03,985 --> 00:29:06,446
into talking complete garbage on TV?

589
00:29:06,780 --> 00:29:07,948
It's not garbage

590
00:29:08,114 --> 00:29:09,574
and he'll be very angry
or very happy

591
00:29:09,699 --> 00:29:11,451
depending on
whether I'm right or not.

592
00:29:11,576 --> 00:29:14,204
Right about what?
There is no diamond...

593
00:29:17,082 --> 00:29:19,251
I see. A trap.
Very clever.

594
00:29:20,710 --> 00:29:22,170
- A trap for who?
- Guess.

595
00:29:22,295 --> 00:29:23,672
- Just tell me.
- Guess.

596
00:29:24,297 --> 00:29:26,466
You know what? Screw you.
You crossed a line already

597
00:29:26,591 --> 00:29:28,510
and now you're playing
guessing games with me?

598
00:29:28,635 --> 00:29:30,428
No, it's just
that I'm not sure myself.

599
00:29:30,553 --> 00:29:32,264
- I have a plan, though.
- Oh, sheepdip.

600
00:29:32,389 --> 00:29:34,391
You are still trying to punish me
for lying to you.

601
00:29:34,516 --> 00:29:37,269
Well, you know what?
I'm sorry. Sorry.

602
00:29:37,394 --> 00:29:39,354
You wanted off this case.
You're off.

603
00:29:39,688 --> 00:29:41,231
You can take a nap
in your thinking room.

604
00:29:41,356 --> 00:29:43,650
We'll solve this case
without you, the right way...

605
00:29:43,775 --> 00:29:45,860
with good, solid detective work.

606
00:29:48,280 --> 00:29:50,323
So I take it you don't wanna
hear my plan?

607
00:29:51,616 --> 00:29:53,493
"No, I don't wanna
hear your plan."

608
00:29:54,703 --> 00:29:56,579
"Sheepdip your plan."

609
00:30:03,985 --> 00:30:05,028
Hi.

610
00:30:08,156 --> 00:30:09,199
Hi, ladies.

611
00:30:10,366 --> 00:30:11,451
We're looking for Sugar.

612
00:30:11,576 --> 00:30:13,453
No Sugar. Just Candy.

613
00:30:14,162 --> 00:30:15,205
You're not Sugar?

614
00:30:15,663 --> 00:30:16,956
No, ma'am. Excuse me.

615
00:30:18,208 --> 00:30:19,500
Yeah, well
we kinda think you are.

616
00:30:19,626 --> 00:30:21,169
Yeah, well thinking's free, flaco.

617
00:30:21,294 --> 00:30:22,462
You're gonna need to come
with us, Sugar.

618
00:30:22,587 --> 00:30:24,339
- What for?
- Because you saw what happened

619
00:30:24,464 --> 00:30:26,132
in that alleyway over on Fremont.

620
00:30:26,257 --> 00:30:27,884
Nah, I was just playing.
I didn't see nothing.

621
00:30:28,218 --> 00:30:30,136
- Okay, still.
- Hey!

622
00:30:30,720 --> 00:30:31,804
Let me go!

623
00:30:35,308 --> 00:30:36,517
Okay.

624
00:30:45,843 --> 00:30:47,095
Here.

625
00:30:54,143 --> 00:30:55,311
Thanks.

626
00:30:56,771 --> 00:30:57,939
No problem.

627
00:30:58,648 --> 00:30:59,732
I'm waiting.

628
00:31:01,692 --> 00:31:03,486
Okay.
I'm at the bridge.

629
00:31:04,278 --> 00:31:05,446
Cigarette break.

630
00:31:06,531 --> 00:31:08,157
Limo comes in off Fremont.

631
00:31:08,282 --> 00:31:09,534
I'm about to go over
and talk business

632
00:31:09,659 --> 00:31:11,828
only this pickup truck comes in
behind him and blocks the limo.

633
00:31:12,412 --> 00:31:15,039
I'm thinking, "uh-oh".
So I-I get up out of sight.

634
00:31:16,207 --> 00:31:17,250
And what kinda truck?

635
00:31:17,583 --> 00:31:20,837
It was a truck.
Uh, green or...

636
00:31:21,921 --> 00:31:24,966
or... or brown or blue.

637
00:31:25,550 --> 00:31:26,801
Not... not red.

638
00:31:26,968 --> 00:31:30,805
And, uh, a white guy gets out
and... and says, "Hey".

639
00:31:30,930 --> 00:31:32,014
What'd he look like?

640
00:31:32,348 --> 00:31:35,184
A white guy.
He had a-a black hat on.

641
00:31:35,309 --> 00:31:37,145
- A white guy in a black hat?
- Yeah.

642
00:31:37,353 --> 00:31:39,147
Yeah, and then he...
he walks up to the limo.

643
00:31:39,272 --> 00:31:40,314
The limo driver gets out.

644
00:31:40,440 --> 00:31:41,649
He's like, "Hey, what the hell
are you doing here?"

645
00:31:41,858 --> 00:31:42,984
"Black hat don't say nothing."

646
00:31:43,109 --> 00:31:44,986
"He just pulls out a piece
and boom!"

647
00:31:45,820 --> 00:31:47,780
Shoots the driver three times

648
00:31:47,947 --> 00:31:50,283
and then he walks
over to the limo door

649
00:31:50,408 --> 00:31:52,201
"and drags out this big fat guy."

650
00:31:52,368 --> 00:31:53,619
"Get out", he says.

651
00:31:53,744 --> 00:31:56,372
And then he... he walks him
to the back of the truck

652
00:31:56,497 --> 00:32:00,084
and makes him climb in the back
and the fat dude's calm.

653
00:32:00,209 --> 00:32:03,713
He's like, "You know,
okay, we can negotiate."

654
00:32:03,838 --> 00:32:05,339
And then boom!

655
00:32:05,465 --> 00:32:08,301
Black hat just...
Shoots him in the head.

656
00:32:08,426 --> 00:32:10,470
"And then he... he puts
a tarp over him"

657
00:32:11,053 --> 00:32:13,473
"and then he jumps back
into his car and he takes off"

658
00:32:13,598 --> 00:32:15,558
"and that's all that happened.
Th-that was it."

659
00:32:16,392 --> 00:32:17,435
Okay.

660
00:32:19,812 --> 00:32:21,063
Do you recognize this guy?

661
00:32:22,398 --> 00:32:24,567
Yeah.
That's the... the fat dude.

662
00:32:26,277 --> 00:32:28,571
- He was dead, you think?
- "Yeah, he's dead."

663
00:32:35,870 --> 00:32:38,414
- So this is about ice?
- Ice? What do you mean?

664
00:32:39,457 --> 00:32:41,334
Well, the TV said that the... the fat dude

665
00:32:41,459 --> 00:32:43,461
had, like a 10-million-dollar diamond.

666
00:32:45,463 --> 00:32:46,714
Yeah, it's a theory.

667
00:33:09,570 --> 00:33:12,406
Um... it's very late.

668
00:33:13,366 --> 00:33:15,284
I w... I wouldn't
intrude like this, uh...

669
00:33:15,409 --> 00:33:18,371
only we have an important lead
perhaps Nadine can help us with.

670
00:33:18,829 --> 00:33:19,872
Oh.

671
00:33:19,997 --> 00:33:22,250
Um, yeah, please, of course.

672
00:33:23,334 --> 00:33:24,377
Thank you.

673
00:33:28,548 --> 00:33:31,133
Um, now you told me
that you and your father

674
00:33:31,259 --> 00:33:32,927
talked about everything
together, yeah?

675
00:33:33,219 --> 00:33:34,262
Yes.

676
00:33:34,387 --> 00:33:35,930
Did you ever talk about his work?

677
00:33:36,389 --> 00:33:39,267
Sometimes. But he never mentioned
a diamond

678
00:33:39,392 --> 00:33:40,643
if that's what you're talking about.

679
00:33:40,768 --> 00:33:42,019
The... wh... diamond?

680
00:33:42,144 --> 00:33:44,605
It said on the news
they were looking for a diamond

681
00:33:44,730 --> 00:33:45,773
Mr. Dublin had with him.

682
00:33:45,898 --> 00:33:49,443
- Uh, yeah, that's... that's just all talk.
- See? I told you.

683
00:33:49,610 --> 00:33:52,280
I mean, there's a diamond all right.
So I hear, anyhow.

684
00:33:52,405 --> 00:33:54,949
Uh, Dublin keeps it hidden
in his false tooth apparently

685
00:33:55,074 --> 00:33:56,534
but nobody knew about it
so I can't see

686
00:33:56,659 --> 00:33:58,160
how anybody could've been
going after it.

687
00:33:58,286 --> 00:34:01,831
No. No, I think this is all connected
to a court case.

688
00:34:03,124 --> 00:34:05,585
Did your dad ever mention
Brixham Lake?

689
00:34:06,127 --> 00:34:07,169
No.

690
00:34:07,670 --> 00:34:10,089
You sure? Brixham Lake.

691
00:34:10,881 --> 00:34:12,133
Think hard.

692
00:34:14,427 --> 00:34:16,095
What about Gale Bertram?

693
00:34:16,887 --> 00:34:18,055
No.

694
00:34:18,472 --> 00:34:19,557
Oh.

695
00:34:21,058 --> 00:34:22,476
Well, that's disappointing.

696
00:34:22,977 --> 00:34:24,312
I was sure...

697
00:34:28,232 --> 00:34:29,609
Oh, well. Uh...

698
00:34:31,360 --> 00:34:32,445
Can't win 'em all.

699
00:34:34,238 --> 00:34:35,406
That's it?

700
00:34:35,740 --> 00:34:39,535
Believe me, uh, I know
it's... it's hard to deal with

701
00:34:39,660 --> 00:34:41,370
but, um...

702
00:34:41,787 --> 00:34:44,040
You know, sometimes
the bad guys just get away.

703
00:34:44,790 --> 00:34:47,251
But you said you'd catch them.
You guaranteed it.

704
00:35:12,401 --> 00:35:15,404
Hey, Lisbon.
How's the detective work doing?

705
00:35:15,571 --> 00:35:17,406
"Eh, doing okay."

706
00:35:17,531 --> 00:35:18,574
Well, I tell you what.

707
00:35:18,699 --> 00:35:20,368
Come over and meet me
at Nadine Russo's house

708
00:35:20,493 --> 00:35:22,036
and I'll show you
something exciting.

709
00:35:24,163 --> 00:35:25,247
Lisbon?

710
00:35:26,082 --> 00:35:27,208
You there?

711
00:35:27,958 --> 00:35:29,001
Lisbon?

712
00:35:36,425 --> 00:35:37,802
- Hi.
- Hi.

713
00:35:39,345 --> 00:35:40,554
I'm very impressed.

714
00:35:41,222 --> 00:35:43,474
Proud and impressed.
You got the right answer.

715
00:35:43,599 --> 00:35:45,142
No real proof, I expect.

716
00:35:45,434 --> 00:35:46,477
Just guessing, huh?

717
00:35:46,602 --> 00:35:48,979
Hmm. "Kettle to pot.
Hello. Come in, pot."

718
00:35:49,438 --> 00:35:51,691
We have a witness who saw
a white male in a truck

719
00:35:51,816 --> 00:35:53,567
who seemed to recognize
Pete Russo.

720
00:35:53,734 --> 00:35:55,569
- Guesswork.
- Do you have proof?

721
00:35:56,153 --> 00:35:59,115
Relax, wait a while and, uh, we shall
soon be taken directly

722
00:35:59,240 --> 00:36:00,574
to the proof you seek.

723
00:37:52,603 --> 00:37:54,480
Keith, stop!

724
00:38:07,701 --> 00:38:09,411
About six months ago

725
00:38:10,454 --> 00:38:11,997
Nadine was...

726
00:38:12,498 --> 00:38:14,667
staying out past curfew

727
00:38:15,459 --> 00:38:17,419
drinking and smoking...

728
00:38:18,921 --> 00:38:21,006
your basic teenage stuff.

729
00:38:22,716 --> 00:38:26,136
So we had a big conversation

730
00:38:26,262 --> 00:38:29,014
about the rules
and... and things got a little heated

731
00:38:30,558 --> 00:38:32,059
you know, as they do.

732
00:38:32,226 --> 00:38:33,269
Yes.

733
00:38:36,188 --> 00:38:37,773
Look, I felt that
she was over-the-line rude

734
00:38:37,898 --> 00:38:41,068
to her mother and me
and I slapped her.

735
00:38:41,360 --> 00:38:42,903
I'm not proud of it.

736
00:38:45,364 --> 00:38:48,284
But it-it was a tap.
Really.

737
00:38:48,868 --> 00:38:50,369
It was no big deal.

738
00:38:53,289 --> 00:38:55,791
Anyway, Nadine told Pete
about it.

739
00:38:57,126 --> 00:38:58,586
He got mad.

740
00:39:01,380 --> 00:39:03,090
Pete and I were buddies.

741
00:39:04,341 --> 00:39:06,552
You know, we could've had a beer
and a discussion

742
00:39:06,677 --> 00:39:08,679
and we could've worked it out.

743
00:39:10,973 --> 00:39:13,100
No, but he comes
into my house...

744
00:39:14,643 --> 00:39:17,354
"And I don't even have a chance
to defend myself!"

745
00:39:18,564 --> 00:39:20,190
"And he sucker punches me"

746
00:39:20,691 --> 00:39:22,776
"and he beat me up badly"

747
00:39:24,320 --> 00:39:27,448
"in front of my wife
and my stepdaughter."

748
00:39:28,949 --> 00:39:32,328
"Put his foot on my neck
and he spat on me..."

749
00:39:33,370 --> 00:39:36,999
- In my own house!
- That's not right.

750
00:39:37,875 --> 00:39:40,878
Even afterwards, I-I...
it's like I'm the one to blame.

751
00:39:41,045 --> 00:39:45,132
You know, Patricia insisted that we make
peace for Nadine's sake.

752
00:39:45,257 --> 00:39:48,802
He does that to me, and I'm
supposed to just swallow it?

753
00:39:49,219 --> 00:39:50,429
Nobody would.

754
00:39:51,055 --> 00:39:54,558
Oh, I thought about ways
to pay him back.

755
00:39:55,184 --> 00:39:57,227
Oh, I thought about it a lot.

756
00:39:59,563 --> 00:40:01,732
But I knew that if I killed him...

757
00:40:03,442 --> 00:40:05,736
Patricia would guess
that it was me.

758
00:40:10,157 --> 00:40:14,495
Unless I could make his death
look like it was just...

759
00:40:15,871 --> 00:40:17,414
a tragic accident.

760
00:40:23,337 --> 00:40:24,380
Sugar?

761
00:40:26,006 --> 00:40:27,424
No, thank you.

762
00:40:27,675 --> 00:40:29,468
- Okay. There you go.
- Thank you.

763
00:40:30,094 --> 00:40:31,345
My pleasure.

764
00:40:32,012 --> 00:40:34,723
We're very sorry
things worked out this way.

765
00:40:35,349 --> 00:40:36,976
You said you'd find out who did it

766
00:40:38,352 --> 00:40:39,561
and you did.

767
00:40:42,773 --> 00:40:43,983
How did you know?

768
00:40:45,025 --> 00:40:46,318
Uh, well, I...

769
00:40:46,860 --> 00:40:50,114
I kinda knew from the first moment
I met you and Keith.

770
00:40:51,240 --> 00:40:53,075
Those little round glasses
with the muscles...

771
00:40:53,200 --> 00:40:55,202
it just didn't seem to match.

772
00:40:55,452 --> 00:40:58,247
Um, indicated
a deeply conflicted personality

773
00:40:58,372 --> 00:40:59,832
a lot of anger and frustration.

774
00:40:59,957 --> 00:41:03,335
Probably uses those, um,
wrist-strengthening things, right?

775
00:41:05,254 --> 00:41:06,964
- Yeah.
- Yeah, um...

776
00:41:07,589 --> 00:41:09,633
never a good sign in a man
over 20.

777
00:41:11,719 --> 00:41:13,345
Obsessive, arrogant

778
00:41:13,512 --> 00:41:17,224
painstaking, uh, secretive
self-righteous. Yeah.

779
00:41:17,349 --> 00:41:18,600
I could go on.

780
00:41:20,352 --> 00:41:22,563
- Yeah.
- Men, huh?

781
00:41:29,611 --> 00:41:31,697
Knew the whole time
and kept it to yourself, eh?

782
00:41:31,822 --> 00:41:34,992
No. No. I only knew when I read
the ransom demand, to be honest

783
00:41:35,451 --> 00:41:37,578
but you lied to me
so fair's fair.

784
00:41:37,703 --> 00:41:39,788
Look, I promise, I will never
lie to you again.

785
00:41:39,913 --> 00:41:42,750
I lied this one time
because I'm concerned.

786
00:41:43,083 --> 00:41:44,543
You're isolating yourself.

787
00:41:44,877 --> 00:41:47,296
You're pulling away from us
from me and the unit.

788
00:41:47,421 --> 00:41:48,464
- You are.
- No.

789
00:41:48,589 --> 00:41:50,966
You are. It's not good.
Wh-what's going on?

790
00:41:55,596 --> 00:41:58,724
Anybody that gets close to me...
bad things happen to them.

791
00:41:58,932 --> 00:42:00,309
A, that's not true

792
00:42:00,434 --> 00:42:02,603
and B, even if it were
I'm a cop.

793
00:42:02,728 --> 00:42:04,897
- It's our job to be in harm's way.
- You're not listening to me.

794
00:42:05,022 --> 00:42:08,233
We're family. What you're doing
is a kind of betrayal.

795
00:42:08,484 --> 00:42:10,694
A surrender. A defeat.
You're letting Red John win.

796
00:42:10,819 --> 00:42:12,863
Oh, please. Please. Really?

797
00:42:12,988 --> 00:42:15,574
Okay. Enough. All right.
No... no more pulling away.

798
00:42:16,033 --> 00:42:17,367
All right? Here, give me a hug.

799
00:42:17,701 --> 00:42:18,786
You don't mean it.

800
00:42:19,495 --> 00:42:20,579
I can tell.

801
00:42:20,704 --> 00:42:22,206
What do you mean you can tell?
You can't tell.

802
00:42:22,539 --> 00:42:24,208
I don't mean it, but you
can't tell that I don't.

803
00:42:24,333 --> 00:42:25,959
- You're guessing.
- Yes, I can tell.

804
00:42:26,585 --> 00:42:27,795
- How?
- I don't know.

805
00:42:27,920 --> 00:42:28,962
I can tell when you're lying now.

806
00:42:29,088 --> 00:42:30,672
- No, you can't tell.
- I-I can, too.

807
00:42:30,923 --> 00:42:34,218
That's nonsense. You're lying.
Liar, liar, pants on fire.

808
00:42:34,384 --> 00:42:35,969
- I'm not lying.
- You are.

